## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to design per-tenant traffic control across data centers, implement dynamic configuration updates, and establish observability—all without service downtime.

## Key Concepts
- **Deterministic hashing**: Ensures consistent per-tenant canary decisions across all service instances
- **Docker Config**: Enables dynamic configuration reloads and zero-downtime rollouts
- **Multi-DC routing**: Traefik handles TLS termination with per-tenant observability across both data centers

## Code Example
```javascript
function isCanary(tenant, percentage) {
  const hash = [...tenant].reduce((acc, char) => (acc * 31 + char.charCodeAt(0)) >>> 0, 0);
  return (hash % 100) < percentage;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design per-tenant QoS with dynamic config in a multi-DC setup.

## Key Concepts
- Traefik RateLimit middleware, Redis store, per-tenant quotas, Docker Config
- TLS termination, hot-reload without restart, cross-DC coordination
- Request tagging with X-Tenant and validation of isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo middleware sketch: extract tenant and enforce quota from Redis
async function enforceTenantRate(req, res, next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const quota = await redisGetQuota(tenant);
  if (quota.allow(req)) return next();
  res.status(429).send('Too Many Requests');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit cross-DC quota drift and recover from Redis replication lag?
- How would you test hot-reload behavior under config churn?
